  <p>I live in Copenhagen. I’m a dad, I listen to a lot of music, and I’ve spent most of my working life trying to understand systems that don’t behave quite the way they’re supposed to.</p>

  <p>I started in a physics lab at the University of Copenhagen, building atomic clocks beyond the quantum limit. I liked the work of finding a frame that made a hard problem simpler. I also acquired a habit of refining an answer long after it was good enough. I still catch myself doing that.</p>

  <p>After the PhD, I spent five years at McKinsey working with European banks. In 2019, I joined Pleo to get closer to the work itself. A payment processor migration became a payments platform, then a wider role across product and engineering. The <a href="/experience/">career history is here</a>.</p>

  <h2>What I keep working on</h2>
  <p>I’m drawn to the points where disciplines meet: a technically clean answer that makes little business sense, a sensible plan that doesn’t survive contact with an organisation, a payment that works until somebody tries it on a Saturday night.</p>
  <p>Working with people has challenged my instincts more than physics did. There are times to step in and times when helping means staying out of the room. I care about building teams that can make good decisions without waiting for me.</p>
  <p>This site is where I write about those experiences and the things I’m still working out. <a href="/posts/physicist-path-to-fintech-leadership/">The physicist’s path</a> is a useful place to begin.</p>

  <h2>Away from work</h2>
  <p>My father played Neil Young, Pink Floyd, and Bruce Springsteen. He would sit through a full side of vinyl without talking. That was part of how I learned to listen.</p>
  <p>These days I return to Sufjan Stevens, Bill Callahan, and Bonnie “Prince” Billy. I still go to shows. I’m trying to read more books and fewer newsletters. <a href="/posts/what-i-listen-to/">I wrote about the music here</a>.</p>

  <p>You can find me on <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/stefanlchristensen">LinkedIn</a>, or follow the writing through <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>.</p>
</Page>
